%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: ad496817cc2ac7b498b32f0fc2de6fb3, type: 3}
  m_Name: R1_01_TheFirstExchange
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::JournalEntry
  title: "1 \xB7 The First Exchange"
  pages:
  - bigIdea: Things stay still (or keep moving) until a net force changes them.
    notes: 'Day one at the ruins. That crate has not moved in a hundred years, and
      two of us leaning on it didn''t change its mind. Not because it''s "heavy",
      exactly: <color=#7d3029>friction</color> pushes back just as hard as we <color=#a26029>push</color>,
      so the forces cancel. Net force zero, motion zero. Newton''s First Law, in
      stone.'
    lookCloser: "Friction can grip with up to  f = \u03BC\xB7m\xB7g = (1/3) \xD7
      3 kg \xD7 29.43 m/s\xB2 \u2248 <color=#7d3029>29.4 N</color>\nTwo pushes: 12
      N + 12 N = <color=#a26029>24 N</color> < <color=#7d3029>29.4 N</color>, so
      friction wins.\nThe tether alone: <color=#267682>24 N</color>. Same story."
    funFact: '"Inertia" comes from the Latin for idleness. Objects really are lazy.'
    marginP1: Pushing harder? We ARE pushing harder.
    marginP2: Then we need a different kind of force...
    diagram:
      show: 1
      objectLabel: 'Crate

        3 kg'
      forces:
      - label: Two pushes
        newtons: 24
        direction: 0
        kind: 0
        unit: N
      - label: Friction
        newtons: 24
        direction: 1
        kind: 2
        unit: N
      caption: 'Balanced: friction matches the push (up to 29.4 N). Net force = 0.'
    sketch: {fileID: 0}
  - bigIdea: An impulse is a big force for a short moment. It changes velocity right
      away.
    notes: The Kinetic Burst doesn't lean on the crate, it SLAMS it. All of the push
      arrives in an instant, so the crate jumps straight to a new speed before friction
      can settle the argument. Heavier things get less speed from the same burst.
    lookCloser: "Impulse  J = F\xB7\u0394t = m\xB7\u0394v,  so  \u0394v = J \xF7
      m\nCrate:  <color=#896923>15 N\xB7s</color> \xF7 3 kg = 5 m/s\nKeystone:  <color=#896923>15
      N\xB7s</color> \xF7 2 kg = 7.5 m/s"
    funFact: A tennis racket touches the ball for about 5 thousandths of a second.
      That's an impulse.
    marginP1: Short and sharp. That's my style.
    marginP2: I'll take steady and from a distance.
    diagram:
      show: 1
      objectLabel: 'Crate

        3 kg'
      forces:
      - label: Burst
        newtons: 15
        direction: 0
        kind: 5
        unit: "N\xB7s"
      caption: "One burst: \u0394v = 15 \xF7 3 = 5 m/s, all at once."
    sketch: {fileID: 0}
  - bigIdea: "Weight is the pull of gravity: W = m\xB7g. Plates feel weight, not
      size."
    notes: The old pressure plates are scales. Each one waits for a certain weight
      before it clicks. Gravity here is three times stronger than back home, so everything
      weighs three times more. A plate counts everything stacked on it, so a player
      on the keystone adds up.
    lookCloser: "W = m \xD7 29.43 m/s\xB2\nPlayer 1 kg \u2192 <color=#8a5a2e>29.4
      N</color>     Keystone 2 kg \u2192 <color=#8a5a2e>58.9 N</color>\nCrate 3 kg
      \u2192 <color=#8a5a2e>88.3 N</color>   (the plate pushes back <color=#42612b>88.3
      N</color>)"
    funFact: "On Earth, g is 9.81 m/s\xB2. This world's 29.43 is exactly three times
      that."
    marginP1: So I'm a 29.4 N hero. Noted.
    marginP2: Stand on the keystone. Instant 88.3 N.
    diagram:
      show: 1
      objectLabel: 'Crate

        3 kg'
      forces:
      - label: Support
        newtons: 88.3
        direction: 2
        kind: 4
        unit: N
      - label: Weight
        newtons: 88.3
        direction: 3
        kind: 3
        unit: N
      caption: The plate pushes up exactly as hard as the crate weighs.
    sketch: {fileID: 0}
  tip: 'One of you stands on the small plate to hold the lift up while the other
    rides to the high perch. Up there, slide the keystone onto its plate. The heavy
    crate on the ledge won''t move for pushing: give it a Burst.'
  sketch: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 2542174c66aa24b48b517bbb15bd2f18, type: 3}
  sketchCaption: 'ALL: every arrow at once.'
